  When attacking by moving into the monster:

 "Your armor is rather cumbersome...  You miss the lichen."

  When attacking by kicking:

 "You kick the lichen.  You kill the lichen!"


  It looks like a kicking Monk avoids the body armor to-hit penalty
while getting the martial arts damage and to-hit bonus!  


  Playing around in explore mode seems to support this.  Could some
source code diver confirm for sure that the monk body armor to-hit
penalty does not apply to kicking?


  


  Notes:

   - To kick: ^D  (or 'k' with numpad turned on.)
   - To repeat the kick and direction: ^A  (repeats last command).

   - Don't kick a footrice without boots on.  
     - Habitual monk players will know this, but monks start with +2 
       gloves so hitting with martial arts is generally safe.

   - In addition to the body armor penalty, monks get a (separate?) 
     to-hit bonus when not wearing body armor.
      - From the Monk FAQ: "Weaponless Monks without body armor (or a 
        shield, in 3.4.1) receive a to-hit bonus: 
        2 + (experience level / 3).

   - There is no chance of dealing a "staggering blow" while wearing 
     body armor or a shield, or when hitting with a wielded weapon.
     - Can kicking ever deliver a "staggering blow?"
     - The chance is never higher than 5%.
     - Nor is it possible against 
       - large, huge, or gigantic monsters
         See: <http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/mon2-343.txt>
       - thick-skinned monsters.
         See: <http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/weap-343.txt>

   - This could help keep "never hit with a wielded weapon" conduct.
     - Habitually using kicks to attack will avoid it.
       - a manual version of turning of "autoattack"
